theory.intro:
A cone's surface has two parts: the flat **circular base**, area $\pi r^{2}$, and the **curved surface**, which unrolls into a sector of area $\pi r l$. So $A=\pi r^{2}+\pi r l$, where $l$ is the slant height.

[tikz]% mathsmap-solid {"version":1,"kind":"analytic-cone","radius":1.3,"projectedRadius":0.42,"height":2.2}

\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc,arrows.meta}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Ap) at (0,2.2);
\coordinate (Lp) at (-1.3,0);
\coordinate (Rp) at (1.3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (5.0,2.6);
\draw (-1.27609004,0.08018182) arc (168.99415755:371.00584245:1.3 and 0.42);
\draw[dashed] (1.27609004,0.08018182) arc (11.00584245:168.99415755:1.3 and 0.42);
\draw (Ap)--(1.27609004,0.08018182);
\path (Ap)--(1.27609004,0.08018182) node[midway,right] {$l$};
\draw (Ap)--(-1.27609004,0.08018182);
\draw (O) -- (Rp) node[midway,below] {$r$};
\fill (O) circle (1.2pt);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (1.7,1.0) -- (2.5,1.0) node[midway,above] {unrolls};
\draw (C) -- ($(C)+(200:2.2)$) node[midway,above left] {$l$};
\draw (C) -- ($(C)+(340:2.2)$) node[midway,above right] {$l$};
\draw ($(C)+(200:2.2)$) arc (200:340:2.2);
\node at (5.0,1.5) {$\pi r l$};
\node[below] at (5.0,0.4) {$2\pi r$};
\draw (2.6,-1.3) circle (0.75);
\fill (2.6,-1.3) circle (1.2pt);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (2.6,-1.3) -- (3.35,-1.3) node[midway,above] {$r$};
\node[below] at (2.6,-2.15) {$\pi r^{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

